  "query_preview": "..A Pacific low is making its way into the Great Basin\nthis evening. The resultant southerly flow is drawing deeper\nmoisture northward into Utah, particularly southwest portions of\nthe state. Satellite derived PWs indicate values up to 0.8 inch\nover southwest Utah, with values in the 0.5 to 0.7 inch range\nelsewhere. That moisture combined with instability on the front\nedge of the low is bringing some patchy showers and thunderstorms\nto southwest Utah. \n\nTonight into tomorrow, the low will track roughly northeast as it\nopens up and splits. One piece should retreat back southwest off\nthe coast, while the large piece moves through Utah and into Idaho\nby Tuesday evening. Associated showers and embedded thunderstorms\nwill tend to increase in coverage through Tuesday morning before\nthe area that is most likely to see showers continues to move\nnorthward with the movement of the trough. Thus, precipitation in\nthe late morning through the afternoon will be most widespread\nover northern portions of the forecast area. As the low exits late\nTuesday afternoon into Tuesday evening, coverage of convection\nwill quickly decrease. \n\nFor the remainder of the work week, much of Utah and southwest\nWyoming will stay dry with generally light flow owing to broad\nridging over the area. Southeast Utah, however, is expected to\nretain some monsoonal moisture over the next several days. Most \nof the moisture and instability will be over Colorado, with Utah\non the periphery seeing primarily isolated convection, but if the\nmain ridge nudges a bit more westward than what is indicated in \nguidance, showers and thunderstorms could be noticeably more \nwidespread. Across the area, temperatures are expected to stay \nroughly near seasonal normals through the weekend.",
